1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,014
A metro map shows a whole city
with a few coloured lines.

2
00:00:04,014 --> 00:00:06,990
A stick figure shows a
person with five strokes.

3
00:00:06,990 --> 00:00:10,357
And a chemist can draw a big
molecule with one zigzag.

4
00:00:10,758 --> 00:00:11,658
Three drawings.

5
00:00:11,455 --> 00:00:13,281
Each one drops the
details you already know.

6
00:00:13,731 --> 00:00:15,627
Aarav sees it at his study table.

7
00:00:15,627 --> 00:00:17,646
He is copying octane from the board.

8
00:00:17,646 --> 00:00:20,523
Eight carbons, eighteen
hydrogens, every single bond.

9
00:00:20,873 --> 00:00:24,023
He is still drawing when
the teacher wipes the board.

10
00:00:24,023 --> 00:00:26,993
But her own notes show
octane as one zigzag line.

11
00:00:27,493 --> 00:00:29,123
Same molecule, much faster.

12
00:00:29,123 --> 00:00:32,763
By the end, you will read and
write all three kinds of drawings.

13
00:00:33,413 --> 00:00:35,541
So how do chemists draw a molecule?

14
00:00:35,891 --> 00:00:39,014
The first way is the
complete structural formula.

15
00:00:39,014 --> 00:00:42,304
Every atom is written,
and every bond is a dash.

16
00:00:42,654 --> 00:00:44,646
A single bond is one dash.

17
00:00:44,646 --> 00:00:46,743
A double bond is two dashes.

18
00:00:46,743 --> 00:00:48,516
A triple bond is three.

19
00:00:48,866 --> 00:00:51,610
The second way is the condensed formula.

20
00:00:51,610 --> 00:00:56,492
We drop the dashes and group the
atoms on each carbon, like CH three.

21
00:00:56,892 --> 00:00:59,493
The third way is the bond line formula.

22
00:00:59,493 --> 00:01:00,393
Only lines.

23
00:01:00,386 --> 00:01:03,181
Carbon and hydrogen
are not written at all.

24
00:01:03,581 --> 00:01:05,487
Three drawings of one molecule.

25
00:01:05,487 --> 00:01:08,853
Like a full photo, a short
note, and a quick sketch.

26
00:01:09,503 --> 00:01:13,390
Let's draw butane all three
ways, on our molecule bench.

27
00:01:13,740 --> 00:01:17,655
First, four carbon atoms join
in a row with single bonds.

28
00:01:18,055 --> 00:01:19,884
Each carbon needs four bonds.

29
00:01:19,884 --> 00:01:21,997
So hydrogens fill every free hand.

30
00:01:21,997 --> 00:01:23,405
Ten hydrogens in all.

31
00:01:23,405 --> 00:01:25,234
This is the complete formula.

32
00:01:25,634 --> 00:01:26,571
Now squeeze.

33
00:01:26,571 --> 00:01:30,552
Each end carbon holds three
hydrogens, so we write CH three.

34
00:01:30,552 --> 00:01:33,190
Each middle carbon holds two, CH two.

35
00:01:33,590 --> 00:01:37,767
That gives CH three, CH
two, CH two, CH three.

36
00:01:37,767 --> 00:01:39,410
The condensed formula.

37
00:01:39,810 --> 00:01:41,457
Now drop every letter.

38
00:01:41,457 --> 00:01:45,030
Keep only the carbon to
carbon bonds, as a zigzag.

39
00:01:45,030 --> 00:01:46,035
Three lines.

40
00:01:46,035 --> 00:01:48,863
This is the bond line formula of butane.

41
00:01:49,513 --> 00:01:51,602
Here are the rules of bond line drawings.

42
00:01:52,002 --> 00:01:55,926
Every corner is a carbon, and
every line end is a carbon.

43
00:01:56,326 --> 00:01:57,863
Hydrogens are not drawn.

44
00:01:57,863 --> 00:02:02,029
Each carbon has four bonds, so the
missing bonds are all to hydrogen.

45
00:02:02,430 --> 00:02:06,274
Other atoms, like oxygen or
chlorine, are always written.

46
00:02:06,274 --> 00:02:08,807
A double bond is drawn as two lines.

47
00:02:09,207 --> 00:02:10,892
Rings are just shapes.

48
00:02:10,892 --> 00:02:12,841
A triangle is cyclopropane.

49
00:02:12,841 --> 00:02:14,791
A pentagon is cyclopentane.

50
00:02:14,791 --> 00:02:16,634
A hexagon is cyclohexane.

51
00:02:17,284 --> 00:02:20,669
Now let's read a bond line
drawing, link by link.

52
00:02:21,019 --> 00:02:21,919
Link one.

53
00:02:21,836 --> 00:02:23,780
Count corners and line ends.

54
00:02:23,780 --> 00:02:25,415
That gives the carbons.

55
00:02:26,165 --> 00:02:27,065
Link two.

56
00:02:26,952 --> 00:02:29,514
At each carbon, count the lines drawn.

57
00:02:30,264 --> 00:02:31,164
Link three.

58
00:02:31,072 --> 00:02:34,049
Hydrogens on that carbon
equal four minus the lines.

59
00:02:34,049 --> 00:02:35,814
A double line counts as two.

60
00:02:36,564 --> 00:02:37,464
Link four.

61
00:02:37,197 --> 00:02:38,876
Add the hydrogens on every carbon.

62
00:02:39,626 --> 00:02:40,526
Link five.

63
00:02:40,426 --> 00:02:41,650
Write the formula.

64
00:02:41,650 --> 00:02:45,136
This branched drawing has five
carbons and twelve hydrogens.

65
00:02:45,136 --> 00:02:46,323
C five H twelve.

66
00:02:47,073 --> 00:02:49,719
But paper is flat, and molecules are not.

67
00:02:49,719 --> 00:02:51,317
Methane is a tetrahedron.

68
00:02:51,317 --> 00:02:52,787
How do we show depth?

69
00:02:53,137 --> 00:02:54,424
Chemists use wedges.

70
00:02:54,424 --> 00:02:56,377
A plain line lies in the page.

71
00:02:56,777 --> 00:02:59,762
A solid wedge comes out
of the page, towards you.

72
00:02:59,762 --> 00:03:02,834
A dashed wedge goes behind
the page, away from you.

73
00:03:03,234 --> 00:03:06,217
Side by side, the flat
drawing hides the shape.

74
00:03:06,217 --> 00:03:07,966
The wedge drawing shows it.

75
00:03:07,966 --> 00:03:11,189
Two bonds in the page,
one towards you, one away.

76
00:03:11,589 --> 00:03:12,652
Pause and think.

77
00:03:12,652 --> 00:03:16,023
In this wedge drawing, which
hydrogen points away from you?

78
00:03:19,223 --> 00:03:21,090
The one on the dashed wedge!

79
00:03:21,090 --> 00:03:22,809
Dashes go behind the page.

80
00:03:22,809 --> 00:03:24,714
Solid wedges come towards you.

81
00:03:25,364 --> 00:03:26,317
Remember this.

82
00:03:26,317 --> 00:03:28,135
Three drawings, one molecule.

83
00:03:28,135 --> 00:03:29,597
The atoms never change.

84
00:03:30,297 --> 00:03:32,065
Now, one worked example.

85
00:03:32,065 --> 00:03:35,393
Here is a bond line
drawing with OH at one end.

86
00:03:35,393 --> 00:03:38,021
Find its condensed and molecular formula.

87
00:03:38,321 --> 00:03:39,221
Step one.

88
00:03:39,107 --> 00:03:40,679
Count corners and ends.

89
00:03:40,679 --> 00:03:41,657
Five carbons.

90
00:03:41,657 --> 00:03:45,376
The OH is written, so it is
joined to the last carbon.

91
00:03:46,126 --> 00:03:47,026
Step two.

92
00:03:46,800 --> 00:03:49,721
The far end carbon has one
line, so three hydrogens.

93
00:03:49,721 --> 00:03:53,398
The three middle carbons have two
lines each, so two hydrogens each.

94
00:03:54,148 --> 00:03:55,048
Step three.

95
00:03:54,964 --> 00:03:58,356
The carbon next to OH has
two lines, so two hydrogens.

96
00:03:58,356 --> 00:04:00,176
Three, plus six, plus two.

97
00:04:00,176 --> 00:04:01,809
Eleven hydrogens on carbon.

98
00:04:02,559 --> 00:04:03,459
Step four.

99
00:04:03,329 --> 00:04:04,929
Add the hydrogen in OH.

100
00:04:04,929 --> 00:04:06,423
Twelve hydrogens in all.

101
00:04:07,173 --> 00:04:11,696
Condensed, it is CH three,
CH two four times, OH.

102
00:04:11,696 --> 00:04:16,322
Shorter still, CH three,
bracket CH two, four, OH.

103
00:04:16,322 --> 00:04:19,061
Its formula is C five H twelve O.

104
00:04:19,711 --> 00:04:21,172
Now, the exam trap.

105
00:04:21,522 --> 00:04:24,122
Students count only the
corners of a zigzag.

106
00:04:24,122 --> 00:04:27,724
Three lines, two corners, so
they say two or three carbons.

107
00:04:28,124 --> 00:04:29,024
Wrong.

108
00:04:28,717 --> 00:04:31,017
The two line ends are carbons too.

109
00:04:31,017 --> 00:04:33,056
Three lines means four carbons.

110
00:04:33,056 --> 00:04:34,202
That is butane.

111
00:04:34,852 --> 00:04:36,388
Where is this asked?

112
00:04:36,388 --> 00:04:41,442
Boards and entrance tests ask you to
turn a bond line drawing into a formula,

113
00:04:41,442 --> 00:04:45,042
expand a condensed formula,
or read a wedge drawing.

114
00:04:45,642 --> 00:04:46,909
Exam level now.

115
00:04:46,909 --> 00:04:51,506
A hexagon with one double line
inside is drawn in bond line form.

116
00:04:51,506 --> 00:04:53,708
What is its molecular formula?

117
00:04:53,708 --> 00:04:55,029
Pause and solve.

118
00:05:00,429 --> 00:05:02,028
The answer is B.

119
00:05:02,028 --> 00:05:03,379
C six H ten.

120
00:05:03,879 --> 00:05:05,781
Six corners, so six carbons.

121
00:05:05,781 --> 00:05:09,315
Four of them have two lines
each, so two hydrogens each.

122
00:05:09,315 --> 00:05:10,363
That is eight.

123
00:05:10,863 --> 00:05:15,410
The two carbons of the double bond
have three lines each, so one hydrogen

124
00:05:15,410 --> 00:05:16,310
each.

125
00:05:15,958 --> 00:05:17,575
Eight plus two is ten.

126
00:05:18,075 --> 00:05:21,386
A, C six H twelve,
forgets the double bond.

127
00:05:21,386 --> 00:05:25,246
C, C six H six, is benzene
with three double bonds.

128
00:05:25,246 --> 00:05:27,376
D is a chain, not a ring.

129
00:05:27,976 --> 00:05:29,516
Here is your recap.

130
00:05:29,516 --> 00:05:31,099
Screenshot this card.

131
00:05:34,298 --> 00:05:36,485
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

132
00:05:36,885 --> 00:05:37,785
One.

133
00:05:37,364 --> 00:05:39,770
Write the condensed formula of propane.

134
00:05:40,570 --> 00:05:41,470
Two.

135
00:05:41,041 --> 00:05:44,423
In a bond line drawing, what
does a corner stand for?

136
00:05:45,223 --> 00:05:46,123
Three.

137
00:05:45,685 --> 00:05:47,480
What does a dashed wedge tell you?

138
00:05:48,380 --> 00:05:53,145
Next time: why do chemists sort millions
of carbon compounds into families?

139
00:05:53,145 --> 00:05:56,387
We find out in classification
of organic compounds.
